{"description":"`validUntil` existed and nothing acted on it: `eka status` printed how many knowledge items were unbounded, but no command named one, and CI had no way to notice. Stale context is the best-documented failure mode for agent-facing knowledge — a stale document makes an agent confidently wrong, where a human patches over it from experience — so a horizon that is only counted is decoration. Making it actionable is the one item in this container that adds value to a user rather than to the repositories.","acceptanceCriteria":"- `eka status --stale` lists every item past its `validUntil`: canonical form, the date, days overdue, Engineering Domain; deterministic order (most overdue first, then canonical form).\n- `eka status --fail-on-stale` exits 1 with the reason on stderr when anything lapsed, 0 otherwise, for CI use.\n- An item with no declared horizon is never stale and never listed: absence is the honest third state.\n- The verdict, the day arithmetic and the boundary come from eka-core's `FreshnessFor`; the listing and the advisory count line name the same items.\n- `--json` emits the same rows (schema-additive) and still exits 1 under `--fail-on-stale`.\n- A store failure is an error, never an empty list: a gate must not read 'could not compute' as 'nothing lapsed'."}